==Latin==

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|la|ine-pro|*sinos}}; akin to {{cog|sq|gji||breast, bosom}}.<ref>{{cite-book|2008|{{w|Michiel de Vaan}}|Etymological Dictionary of Latin and the Other Italic Languages|location=Leiden|publisher={{w|Brill Publishers|Brill}}||567}}</ref>

The mathematical sense ‘chord of an arc, sine’ was introduced in the 12th century by {{w|Gherardo of Cremona}} as a {{semantic loan|la|ar|جَيْب||chord, sine|nocap=1}} (ultimately a loan from {{der|la|sa|ज्या||bowstring}}) by confusion with {{m|ar|جَيْب||bosom, fold in a garment}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{la-IPA}}

====Noun====
{{la-noun|sinus<4>}}

# {{lb|la|mostly|poetic}} a [[bent]] [[surface]]; a [[curve]], [[fold]], [[hollow]]
# {{lb|la|literal}} the [[hang]]ing [[fold]] of a [[toga]] over the [[breast]]; a [[pocket]], [[lap]]
#: {{syn|la|gremium}}
## {{lb|la|transferred sense}}
### a [[purse]], [[money]], {{ngd|which was carried in the [[bosom]] of the [[toga]]}}
### {{lb|la|poetic}} a [[garment]]
### the [[bosom]], [[breast]]
###: {{syn|la|pectus}}
## {{lb|la|figurative}}
### the [[bosom]] for [[love]], [[protection]], [[asylum]]
### the [[interior]], [[inmost]] part of a thing
### a [[power]], [[possession]] of someone
### a [[hiding place]], [[place]] of [[concealment]]; a [[secret]] [[feeling]]
# a [[gulf]], [[bay]], [[bight]]
## the [[land]] lying on or a [[point]] of [[land]] that helps to form a [[gulf]]
## a [[basin]], [[hollow]], [[valley]]
## {{lb|la|Medieval Latin}} a [[fjord]]
# {{lb|la|Medieval Latin|mathematics}} the [[chord]] of an [[arc]]; a [[sine]]

